===Noun===
{{en-plural noun}}

# The [[ratio]] of the [[probability]] of an [[event]] [[happening]] to that of it not happening.
#* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|1|I|ii|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=0HFGOmnsvAMC&pg=PP24|A thouſand Perſean horſemen are at hand,<br>Sent from the King to ouercome vs all. {{...}}<br>A thouſand horſmen? We fiue hundred foote?<br>An '''ods''' too great, for vs to ſtand againſt: {{...}}}}
#: {{ux|en|I'd say the '''odds''' are strongly in favor of the sun rising tomorrow morning.}}
# The ratio of [[winnings]] to [[stake]] in betting situations.
#: {{ux|en|I looked at the '''odds''' given by all bookmakers.}}
# {{lb|en|chess}} An [[advantage]] given to a weaker [[opponent]] in order to [[equalize]] the game when playing casually, usually by removing one of the stronger player's pieces or by giving the weaker player more [[time]].
#: {{ux|en|She beat me with knight '''odds''' but lost with rook '''odds'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|The grandmaster gave his opponents significant time '''odds''', of one minute versus ten minutes.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The British Chess Magazine|passage= The resulting match of fourteen games was won by Mr. Maurian, who had scored all the Knight-'''odds''' parties and the majority of the even-term ones!|year=1913|page=51|publisher=Trubner & Company|volume=33}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Simon & Schuster Pocket Book of Chess|passage=Nowadays, giving material '''odds''' in this way is rather rare, but the advent of the chess clock has made it possible for strong players to give time '''odds''' — taking one or two minutes for all their moves, for example, and allowing their opponents five minutes or more.|year=1989|author=Raymond Keene|page=183|isbn=9780671679248|publisher=Simon and Schuster Books for Young Readers}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Chess and Machine Intuition|passage=Harry Golombek, who had returned from Argentina with the British chess team but spent two years in the infantry before joining B.P., occasionally played chess with Turing, giving Queen '''odds''' in order to make the game more equal.|year=1998|author=George W. Atkinson|page=35|isbn=9781871516449|publisher=Intellect}}
#* {{quote-web|en|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220526174540/https://fivethirtyeight.com/features/the-world-chess-championship-comes-down-to-the-final-game/|work={{w|FiveThirtyEight}}|passage=And finally, if those are tied, they'll play a final sudden-death game, using a format known as armageddon. In armageddon, black gets "time '''odds'''": White gets five minutes while black gets just four, but a draw counts as a win for black.|title=The World Chess Championship Comes Down To The Final Game|date=2016-11-27|author=Oliver Roeder}}
#* {{quote-web|en|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20210506210109/https://thewire.in/sport/underrepresentation-women-chess-batumi-olympiad|work={{w|The Wire (India)|The Wire}}|passage=Fischer described all female professionals' play at his time to be that of "beginners", and went on to boast about hypothetically beating any female chess player with knight '''odds''' (when challenged to the same, though, he didn't respond).|title=Queens on the Board, Pawns in the Sport – the Underrepresentation of Women in Chess|date=2018-09-23|author=Binit Priyaranjan}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Chess Board Options|passage=The most important handicap historically is knight '''odds''', because it produces play more similar to a normal game than handicaps like f7 and multiple moves, while still being a suitable handicap for rather strong players.|year=2021|author=Larry Kaufman|isbn=9789056919368|publisher=New in Chess}}
#* {{quote-web|en|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20221217212113/https://dotesports.com/chess/news/nakamura-wins-2022-fischer-random-chess-championship-carlsen-slides-to-top-four-finish|work=Dot Esports|passage=Here, Nakamura took the white pieces and the time '''odds''' with the mandatory win requirement, and managed to break Nepomniachtchi's resistance to clinch the title.|title=Nakamura wins 2022 Fischer Random Chess Championship, Carlsen slides to top-four finish|date=2022-10-31|author=Luci Kelemen}}

{{head|en|noun form}}

# {{plural of|en|odd}}

====Usage notes====
* The word "odds" was formerly sometimes used with a [[singular]] verb, e.g. (Stephen S. Foster) "If there be any difference in the two crimes, the odds is in favor of the foreign enslaver."

